From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: build <build@localhost>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 17:23:17 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 867/867] wifi: mt6797: keep the phy across a WLAN restart

A restart unregistered the mac80211 layer and registered a new one, so
phy0 became phy1 and wlan0 a new interface. systemd-rfkill writes a
state change only when it exits, 5 s after the last event, and drops the
queued write of an rfkill device that goes away; it applies the saved
state to a device that appears. An unblock followed within 5 s by the
restart it set off was lost that way: the new phy came up soft-blocked,
and that state was saved for the next boot.

Keep the phy instead. The restart halts the mac layer: an associated
interface is disconnected while the firmware is still there, the join
and the scan close as a stop closes them, and the interrupt, the work
items and the queues are released before CONSYS frees the HIF. When the
new firmware is ready, CONSYS hands the kept phy back to reg, which
starts the lifetime group from zero under the wiphy lock and configures
the firmware as a re-attach does. wlan0 stays, and rfkill sees no
device come or go.

Taking a closed interface up now succeeds: it asks for the restart once
per lifetime and comes up at once; scans are refused until the new
lifetime is ready, and the supplicant retries them. With CONSYS built
in there is no restart and the up is refused as before.

The mac layer and CONSYS must be loaded together: the ops structure
gains the halt.
